{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2131201979/13/codegen_prompts/5.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/13`\n- 题号：`5`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed2131201979/13/5.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"5\">\n<description>\n在包含多个嵌套矩形框的网格中，根据每层框的颜色在一个独立的颜色序列条中的排列顺序，决定该层框内部的填充内容是保留还是清除，从而产生选择性\"剥洋葱\"效果。\n\nInput structure:\n- 背景为black。网格分为左右两部分，由一列grey分隔线隔开。\n- 右侧为主画布，包含一组同心嵌套的矩形框（3-5层），每层框的颜色不同，框线宽1像素。最内层框的内部填充了一种特殊颜色（与所有框色不同）的实心区域。\n- 左侧为颜色序列条：一列纵向排列的单像素色块，从上到下排列了主画布中所有框的颜色（顺序可能与嵌套层级不同）。序列条中某些颜色像素的右侧紧邻一个yellow标记像素，另一些则没有。\n\nRule:\n- 读取左侧颜色序列条，识别哪些框颜色带有yellow标记。\n- 在右侧主画布中，对于带yellow标记的框层：保留该框线及其内部所有内容不变。\n- 对于不带yellow标记的框层：移除该层框线，将原框线位置替换为该框内侧下一层的颜色（如果下一层也被移除，则继续向内查找，直到找到一个保留层或最内层填充色）。\n- 最终效果是：被标记的框层保留，未标记的框层\"透明化\"消失，内部内容向外可见。\n\nKey insight:\n解题需要三层推理：第一层是发现左侧序列条是一个查找表，将颜色映射到保留/移除决策；第二层是理解\"移除框线\"意味着用内层颜色填充而非简单变黑；第三层是处理连续多层被移除时的递归填充逻辑——最终的视觉效果类似于从嵌套结构中有选择地抽取特定层。\n</description>\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/dbff022c, eval/581f7754, eval/88e364bc, train/e3fe1151, eval/8b7bacbf, train/34cfa167, eval/89565ca0, eval/13e47133\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dbff022c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3826\nmechanism=0.4536\nlexical=0.0106\ncode_feature=0.6000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, operation:flood_fill, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/dbff022c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/dbff022c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill hollow sprites' interiors using a color-palette legend on the grid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several hollow sprites with distinct border colors.\n- A small 2×N or N×2 palette block sits on one grid edge; one row/column lists border colors, the other lists corresponding fill colors.\n\nRule:\n- Locate the palette legend on a grid edge and read it as a border→fill color mapping.\n- Find enclosed bg regions (holes) inside sprites that don't touch the grid boundary.\n- For each hole, identify the most common adjacent border color, look it up in the palette, and fill the hole with the mapped fill color.\n\nKey insight: The palette's edge position and orientation determine reading direction; border colors in the palette uniquely key into fill colors.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Use a color palette legend on the grid edge to fill sprites' interiors based on their border color mapping.\",\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"hollow sprites with colored borders s\",\n  \"dst\": \"interior of same sprite d\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"d.fill_color == palette_lookup(s.border_color)\",\n  \"path\": \"\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill d with palette[s.border_color]\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"palette is 2xN or Nx2 block on grid edge; read direction based on edge position: top edge reads top-to-bottom, bottom edge reads bottom-to-top, left edge reads left-to-right; first row/column is border color, second is fill color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"Step 1: locate 2xN or Nx2 palette legend on grid edge; Step 2: build color mapping (border -> fill) based on reading direction; Step 3: for each hollow sprite, lookup border color in palette and fill interior with corresponding fill color\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"color-mapping\",\n    \"lookup-table\",\n    \"flood-fill\",\n    \"border-detection\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The challenge is correctly reading the palette legend based on its edge position and orientation\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"581f7754\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3802\nmechanism=0.4676\nlexical=0.0115\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:axis, object:frame, object:marker, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/581f7754.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/581f7754.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide multi-color sprites to align their marker pixels with matching isolated border anchors.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Multi-color sprites in the interior; each has a minority-color \"marker\" pixel.\n- Isolated border pixels (non-bg with all 4-neighbors bg) act as anchors.\n\nRule:\n- Match each sprite to the border anchor sharing its marker color.\n- Top/bottom-edge anchor (non-corner): slide sprite horizontally so marker column = anchor column.\n- Left/right-edge anchor (or corner): slide sprite vertically so marker row = anchor row.\n\nKey insight: The anchor's edge position determines the slide axis — top/bottom means horizontal, left/right (or corner) means vertical.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"multi-color sprite s with a unique marker color (minority color inside s)\",\n  \"dst\": \"align to isolated border pixel d (same marker color)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.marker.color == d.color\",\n  \"transform\": \"translate s: if d on top/bottom edge → align col(s.marker) to col(d); else → align row(s.marker) to row(d); clip out-of-bounds pixels\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3782\nmechanism=0.4768\nlexical=0.0130\ncode_feature=0.4286\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:marker, object:rectangle, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:direction, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/88e364bc.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\nRule:\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"summary\": \"Yellow pixels inside colored-border regions move in the direction indicated by the red pixel in the legend with matching background color, stopping at region boundary.\",\n  \"type\": \"move_along_path\",\n  \"src\": \"yellow pixels inside colored-border regions s\",\n  \"dst\": null,\n  \"match_cond\": \"\",\n  \"path\": \"move(s, direction=legend.red_pixel_offset) where legend.background_color == s.containing_region.border_color until s.touches(region_boundary)\",\n  \"trajectory\": \"\",\n  \"transform\": \"\",\n  \"symbol_instructions\": \"legend on grid edge with background color C and red pixel indicates: yellow pixels in regions with border color C move in direction from blue center to red pixel\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"\",\n  \"concepts\": [\n    \"legend/control-pixels\",\n    \"region-membership\",\n    \"directional-movement\",\n    \"boundary-collision\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3042\nmechanism=0.4146\nlexical=0.0115\ncode_feature=0.1667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, object:axis, object:marker, object:rectangle, operation:sort_order, relation:inside, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e3fe1151.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e3fe1151.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"four equal corner regions divided by a solid background cross, each with one background-colored blank pixel\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill each blank with the color needed so every corner region has the same color multiset, using the maximum observed count of each color across the regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8b7bacbf\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3314\nmechanism=0.3959\nlexical=0.0111\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:bfs, code:connected_components, object:closed_region, object:frame, object:marker, operation:flood_fill, operation:sort_order, relation:adjacent, relation:inside\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
